		tags = { "Specific works of nonfiction", "Nonfiction of modern era" },
		summary = '**The Structure of Scientific Revolutions** is a 1962 book about the [[History of science|history of science]] by the philosopher Thomas S. Kuhn. Its publication was a landmark event in the [[History of science|history]], [[Philosophy of science|philosophy]], and sociology of science. Kuhn challenged the then prevailing view of progress in science in which scientific progress was viewed as "development-by-accumulation" of accepted facts and theories. Kuhn argued for an episodic model in which periods of conceptual continuity and cumulative progress, referred to as periods of "normal science", were interrupted by periods of revolutionary science. The discovery of "anomalies" accumulating and precipitating revolutions in science leads to new [[Paradigm|paradigms]].',
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				'New paradigms then ask new questions of old data, move beyond the mere "puzzle-solving" of the previous paradigm, alter the rules of the game and change the "map" directing new research. For example, Kuhn\'s analysis of the Copernican Revolution emphasized that, in its beginning, it did not offer more accurate predictions of celestial events, such as planetary positions, than the Ptolemaic system, but instead appealed to some practitioners based on a promise of [[Occam\'s razor|better, simpler solutions]] that might be developed at some point in the future. Kuhn called the core concepts of an ascendant revolution its "paradigms" and thereby launched this word into widespread analogical use in the second half of the 20th century.',
