		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"It is uncertain whether time travel to the past would be physically possible. Such travel, if at all feasible, may give rise to questions of causality. Forward time travel, outside the usual sense of the perception of time, is an extensively observed phenomenon and is well understood within the framework of [[Theory of relativity|special relativity]] and [[Theory of relativity|general relativity]]. However, making one body advance or delay more than a few milliseconds compared to another body is not feasible with current technology. As for backward time travel, it is possible to find solutions in general relativity that allow for it, such as a rotating black hole. Traveling to an arbitrary point in spacetime has very limited support in [[Theoretical physics|theoretical physics]], and is usually connected only with quantum mechanics or wormholes.",
